== Poincaré duality <sec:poincare-duality>
#idx("Poincaré", "duality", target: [@sec:poincare-duality])#idx(
  "duality, Poincaré",
  target: [@sec:poincare-duality],
)

#source(221)In this section, taken from [@bib:Benson1994a], we show that if $k$
is a field, and $H^* (G, k)$ is finitely generated as a module over the subring
generated by homogeneous elements $zeta_1, dots, zeta_r$ (so that these elements
cover the variety of the trivial module) then the complex of projective modules
$times.o.big_(i=1)^r bold(C)_(zeta_i)$ described in
Theorem~@th:multiple-complex-resolution (with $M = k$) is homotopy equivalent to
its dual, suitably shifted in degree. We begin with a general lemma about
homotopy equivalences.

#lemma[
  Suppose $bold(C)$ and $bold(D)$ are finite chain complexes of finitely
  generated projective $k G$-modules, and $f : bold(C) -> bold(D)$ is a chain
  map. Then the following are equivalent.
  + $f$ induces an isomorphism in homology
    $f_* : H_* (bold(C)) limits(arrow.r.long)^tilde.equiv H_* (bold(D))$.
  + $f$ is a homotopy equivalence.
  + There exist decompositions $bold(C) = bold(C)' plus.o bold(P)'$,
    $bold(D) = bold(D)' plus.o bold(Q)'$, where $bold(P)'$ and $bold(Q)'$ are
    exact sequences of projective modules, and the restriction of $f$ to
    $bold(C)'$ is an isomorphism
    $f : bold(C)' limits(arrow.r.long)^tilde.equiv bold(D)'$.
] <lem:homotopy-equivalence>

#proof[
  It is clear that (iii) $=>$ (ii) $=>$ (i), so we shall prove that (i) $=>$
  (iii). Suppose $f : bold(C) -> bold(D)$ induces an isomorphism in homology. By
  adding an exact sequence of projective modules $bold(Q)$ to $bold(C)$ we may
  make $f$ surjective, and still a homology isomorphism. Denote by $bold(P)$ the
  kernel of $f : bold(C) plus.o bold(Q) arrow.r.twohead bold(D)$. The long exact
  sequence in homology shows that $bold(P)$ is an exact sequence of projectives.
  Since projective $k G$-modules are also injective, the sequence
  $ 0 -> bold(P) -> bold(C) plus.o bold(Q) -> bold(D) -> 0 $
  splits, and so $bold(C) plus.o bold(Q) tilde.equiv bold(D) plus.o bold(P)$.
  The result now follows from the Krull–Schmidt theorem for finite chain
  complexes of finitely generated $k G$-modules.
]

Now if $zeta in H^n (G, k)$ is represented by a cocycle
$hat(zeta) : Omega^n k -> k$, we may dualise to obtain a map
$hat(zeta)^* : k -> Omega^(-n) k$. Applying $Omega^n$ to this map, we obtain a
map $Omega^n (hat(zeta)^*) : Omega^n k -> k$. The relationship between this map
and the original map $hat(zeta)$ is given in the following proposition.

#proposition[
  If $zeta in H^n (G, k)$ then
  $ Omega^n (hat(zeta)^*) = (-1)^(n(n+1) slash 2) hat(zeta). $
  There is a map of chain complexes
  $ #dual-truncation $
  inducing an isomorphism on homology.
] <prop:dual-cocycle>

#proof[
  #source(222)We build a commutative diagram
  $ #dual-cocycle-diagram $
  In this diagram, the bottom set of vertical arrows has been filled in using
  the fact that both rows are exact sequences of projective modules, except at
  the ends, and $gamma$ is obtained by restricting the previous map. Now modulo
  maps which factor through a projective module, there is only one dimension of
  maps from $Omega^(-n) k times.o Omega^n k$ to $k$. It follows that $gamma$ is
  some multiple $lambda_n . upright(e v)$ of the evaluation map (regarding
  $Omega^(-n) k$ as the dual of $Omega^n k$). Now the composite of the left-hand
  vertical maps in the above diagram is $Omega^n (hat(zeta)^*)$, and so we have
  $
    Omega^n (hat(zeta)^*) = lambda_n . upright(e v) compose
    (hat(zeta)^* times.o 1) = lambda_n . hat(zeta).
  $
  (There are no maps from $Omega^n k$ to $k$ which factor through a projective
  module).

  It remains to determine the constants $lambda_n$. It is clear that $lambda_n$
  only depends on $n$, and not on $G$ or $zeta$. Since the map
  $zeta |-> lambda_n . zeta$ is an anti-automorphism of $H^* (G, k)$ (duality
  reverses Yoneda composition) we have
  $ lambda_m xi . lambda_n zeta = lambda_(m+n) zeta . xi, $
  so that
  $ lambda_m lambda_n = (-1)^(m n) lambda_(m+n). $
  It thus remains to determine $lambda_1$. Since the transpose inverse of the
  matrix $mat(1, alpha; 0, 1)$ is $mat(1, 0; -alpha, 1)$, we see that
  $lambda_1 = -1$, and so by induction $lambda_n = (-1)^(n(n+1) slash 2)$.

  Now the left-hand map,
  $gamma compose (hat(zeta)^* times.o 1) = (-1)^(n(n+1) slash 2) hat(zeta)$, in
  the above diagram has kernel $L_zeta$, while the right-hand map $hat(zeta)^*$
  has cokernel $L_zeta^*$, and so by passing to the appropriate quotient on the
  top row and subcomplex on the bottom row, we obtain a map of complexes
  $ #dual-quotient $
  and hence the required homology equivalence.
]

#theorem[
  Suppose that $H^* (G, k)$ is finitely generated as a module over the subring
  generated by $zeta_1, dots, zeta_r$ in degrees $n_1, dots, n_r$ with each
  $n_i >= 2$. Then the complex
  $ bold(C) = times.o.big_(i=1)^r bold(C)_(zeta_i) $
  #source(223)is a direct sum of a complex $bold(C)'$ satisfying Poincaré
  duality in formal dimension $s = sum_(i=1)^r (n_i - 1)$,
  $ bold(Hom)_k (bold(C)', k) [s] tilde.equiv bold(C)' $
  and an exact complex $bold(P)'$ of projective modules.
] <th:poincare-duality-complex>

#proof[
  By the proposition, we have homology equivalences
  $ bold(Hom)_k (bold(C)_(zeta_i), k) [n_i - 1] -> bold(C)_(zeta_i). $
  Putting these together, we obtain a homology equivalence
  $ bold(Hom)_k (bold(C), k) [s] tilde.equiv bold(C) $
  By Theorem~@th:multiple-complex-resolution, $bold(C)$ is a finite complex of
  projective modules, and so the theorem now follows by applying
  Lemma~@lem:homotopy-equivalence.
]

#corollary(closed: true)[
  If $bold(C)$ and $s$ are as in the theorem, then the Poincaré series
  $f(t) = sum_(i=0)^s t^i dim_k H^i (Hom_(k G) (bold(C), k))$ satisfies the
  functional equation $t^s f(1 slash t) = f(t)$.
] <cor:poincare-series-duality>
